From 8b8f54084b5677775d720e4ab63bac94c804a5f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 11:13:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Rebuild the bias battery: 10 constructs, 34 items
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Replaces the previous battery to match the specified construct table.

Added:   Illusion of Knowledge (IOK, 4), Recency (REC, 3)
Merged:  overestimation / overplacement / overprecision -> Overconfidence (OVC, 4)
Resized: FOMO 5 -> 4 items
Resourced: every construct now cites the specified parent sources

HER 4 · OVC 4 · FOM 4 · AVL 3 · REC 3 · ANC 3 · CNF 3 · LAV 3 · IOK 4 · REP 3
= 34 items, all 5-point. Total instrument is now 81 items.

Sources were verified before wiring in, and three findings shaped the build:

- Glaser & Weber (2007) measure overconfidence with estimation TASKS (90%
  confidence intervals; percentile self-placement), not Likert items. The OVC
  items render those constructs as agreement statements — recorded as an
  adaptation needing its own validation. Their finding that miscalibration and
  better-than-average are uncorrelated is why OVC and IOK are separate
  constructs here rather than facets of one.
- Park et al. (2013) measure confirmation bias behaviourally (a selective-
  exposure click index), so CNF items adapt the paradigm rather than borrowing
  items. Their 3-item perceived knowledge scale (alpha .81) informs IOK.
- Rozenblit & Keil (2002) measure the illusion as a pre/post difference score,
  which one Likert administration cannot reproduce, and no validated
  illusion-of-knowledge scale exists in finance (Franco Moreno et al., 2025).
  IOK is therefore scored twice: as a subscale, and as a subjective-objective
  calibration gap against the objective literacy score. A positive gap is the
  illusion. Surfaced in the insights, the report and the CSV export.

Pompian (2006) and Nofsinger (2017) are textbooks and are cited as such, for
taxonomy and conceptual definition, never as instruments. Kengatharan &
Kengatharan (2014) dropped its availability and representativeness items at
EFA, so those constructs draw on Waweru et al. (2008) instead.

Second-order grouping now follows Pompian's cognitive/emotional taxonomy.
Learning modules and weekly challenges rewritten for the new constructs.
RESEARCH.md carries the full table and five stated caveats.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J99CXD5HFH9Tpa7mP78gNy
